GREG BRAIL: OK.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.420
Good afternoon.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.930
Thanks for coming.

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:12.030
My name is Greg Brail.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.170
I'm a software
engineer at Google.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.650
I've been part of the team
that works on the Apigee API

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.560
management platform for about
12 years, been at Google

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.060
a little more than two years.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.690
I'll be joined later by Keith
Danekind who works in our field

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.610
and that's going to
help me with some demos.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.810
And the main thing
we're going to talk

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.902
about today is we're
going to talk about APIs.

00:00:29.902 --> 00:00:31.860
We're going to talk about
things that companies

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.380
have done with APIs.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.130
We're going to show
some examples of that.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.780
We're going to talk about the
business for about 10 minutes.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.488
Then we're going to
get into a little bit

00:00:39.488 --> 00:00:41.550
about what API management
technology looks like

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.750
and what people are
able to do with it.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.590
So there's our agenda but better
than look at the agenda slide

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.870
that's just get started.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.810
So these are
examples of companies

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:54.530
who have had real business
results by using APIs,

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:58.520
either by creating API programs
that are used by third parties,

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.420
by creating new digital
experiences with APIs,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.780
or by essentially taking
advantage of the ability

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.290
to use an API as a layer on
top of legacy technology.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.300
For instance, Starbucks
has a mobile app

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.567
that people use
for buying coffee.

00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:15.650
And they also have some
interesting digital wallet

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:16.790
capabilities.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.700
That app runs on an API
that is layered on top

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.900
of Starbucks legacy technology.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.430
I'll get better as this goes on.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.440
Similarly, a company
called Arity is actually

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.390
in the business to
business business.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.920
And what they've
been able to use

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.830
is by having an API
program, rather than

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.260
the old fashioned way of
getting partners signed up that

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.440
takes months and months to
have conversations, and emails,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.990
and PowerPoints, and things.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.210
By having a self-service
API, their partners

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:48.117
can get on board in just a
few days or even a few hours.

00:01:48.117 --> 00:01:49.700
This is an example
of some of the ways

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.440
that customers have
been taking advantage

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.440
of this API technology that
we all know from the internet.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.900
Applying it to their businesses
and getting some real results.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.940
APIs and API management has
been around for a while.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.060
We've been talking about
it for a long time.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.000
The term API of course,
is 40 years old.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.100
I'm really talking
here about a web APIs.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.530
The kind of APIs that
run on the internet.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:15.430
The kind of APIs you use when
you interface with Google Cloud

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.060
or with third party services
like Twitter, or even Facebook.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.160
Most companies that
start out with API

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.300
kind of start out on
the lower left here.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.480
They need to solve some
integration problems.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.490
I need to connect a
new technology platform

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:29.410
to an old technology platform.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.560
I want to make it easy to build
something like a mobile app.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.600
And sort of on the two boxes
on the lower left hand corner,

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.410
this is what people were
doing six or seven years ago.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.800
They were doing things like
putting an API layer in front

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.740
of a legacy app so that mobile
developers could use technology

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.430
they understood
like JSON and HTTP

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.520
to build a new experience
for their existing legacy

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:52.630
technology.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.880
But what started to
happen is for one, just

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.800
having a mobile
app wasn't enough.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.570
Now we have things
like connected devices,

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.700
and smart speakers,
and connected cars,

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.620
and talking refrigerators,
and things like that.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.610
And all of those kinds of
things require some sort of API

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:07.600
behind them.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.330
When you say, OK, Google buying
me tickets for the Lady Gaga

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:12.910
concert, that's only
possible if the company that

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:16.180
selling those tickets
actually has some sort of API

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.890
for Google Home to speak to.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:19.997
So fancy marketing
term but instead

00:03:19.997 --> 00:03:21.580
of talking about
building apps, now we

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.963
talk about creating
digital experiences.

00:03:23.963 --> 00:03:26.380
But the companies that have
really done interesting things

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.160
with APIs have used APIs to
create new business models

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.840
and create marketplaces
and platforms.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.180
And I'll talk about
some examples.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.000
One great example
we have is a Magalu,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.250
formerly known as
Magazine Luiza.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.897
It's the largest
retailer in Brazil.

00:03:40.897 --> 00:03:42.730
They'll be speaking
later at the conference.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.280
We'll put up a list of some
of the sessions later on.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.990
Magalu is a brick
and mortar retailer

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:48.730
with stores all over Brazil.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.380
And a couple of
years ago, they began

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.450
transforming their
business by changing

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.400
from a traditional
retailer to one

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.040
that has very strong
capabilities around the web

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.543
and around mobile apps.

00:03:58.543 --> 00:04:00.460
But in recent years
they've gone even farther.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.080
Now they have a platform.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.020
So third party sellers,
logistics providers,

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:09.640
all kinds of people can both
put products in their stores.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.410
Take advantage of
their network of stores

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:13.000
and their distribution
capabilities

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.740
to sell their products.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.540
As well as build other
ways to integrate

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.880
with their infrastructure.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.279
Essentially, this
makes Magalu the Amazon

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:23.620
of Brazil, which is funny
because Brazil already

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.045
has an Amazon.

00:04:25.045 --> 00:04:26.920
But it's something that's
actually given them

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.570
huge business
results and actually

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:32.000
huge results in their
stock price as well.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.510
Another great example is
Accuweather, also speaking here

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.970
at the conference.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.980
We all know Accuweather
in the United States

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.650
as being the person who the
weather man on television

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.280
gets information from.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.490
But that weather
person on television

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:46.790
is only part of what they do.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:50.140
They also have an API that
gets 40 to 50 billion API

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.690
calls a day from all
sorts of digital devices

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:55.470
and connected devices that
want to show you the weather.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.970
But the interesting thing is
that their business is actually

00:04:57.970 --> 00:04:59.210
much more than that.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.870
There are businesses such
as insurance and financial

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:04.330
services, risk
management, reinsurance,

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:08.320
all kind of businesses like that
that the weather events have

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.630
a big impact on their business.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:14.710
So Accuweather is able to sell
more valuable, basically more

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:17.980
expensive forecast products
tailored to those industries.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.980
And the fact that if they
can have an API platform that

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.850
allows people to sign up and get
those products via self-service

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:28.090
by clicking a button on a
website, signing up for an API,

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:30.430
paying some money,
and using that API

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.950
they can get customers to
use those systems much more

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:36.300
quickly than they
might have in the past.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.730
Last example I'll give
you is Ticketmaster.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.440
In the United
States, Ticketmaster

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.240
is one of the companies
you go to buy tickets.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.630
But an interesting stat
is that at least 10%

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.610
of the traffic of the
numbers of tickets that

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.730
are sold on Ticketmaster
are not sold directly

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:53.760
from Ticketmaster's website
or they're mobile app,

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.110
but from third parties.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:56.910
For instance, there
are hotels who,

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.410
when you make your
hotel reservation

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.368
they say, hey, you'll be
in New York next week.

00:06:00.368 --> 00:06:02.040
How about tickets to "Frozen?"

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.860
These kinds of things
actually happen using API.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.460
And again it's a great example
of a company like Ticketmaster

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.510
using APIs to allow third
parties to drive new businesses

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:15.140
on top of their platform.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:17.600
The reason why this
kind of thing can work

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.460
is that these companies treat
APIs not as a bit of technology

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.280
or about, hey, isn't it cool
that I can send you JSON

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:26.810
and get tickets to "Frozen."

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:30.110
But actually, that they think
about their APIs as products

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:31.710
that they sell to a customer.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.880
They have to think about who
the customer is for their API.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.920
So Magalu has to
think about if I'm

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:40.250
going to offer an API
for third party vendors

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.190
to sell stuff in my
network of stores

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.730
what kind of technologies
do those vendors have?

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.480
What are they going
to have to do in order

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.490
to actually get on my platform?

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:51.800
What can I do to
reduce the friction?

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.780
And how do I keep track
of that so it actually

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.462
affects my business.

00:06:55.462 --> 00:06:57.170
Similarly, Accuweather
has to think about

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.450
if people are going to build
mobile apps or other kind

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:01.850
of digital experiences
on my services

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:03.770
what kind of technology
do I have to offer?

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:06.270
What kind of API do
you have to offer?

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.210
In other words,
these companies that

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.750
have done successful things
in their business with APIs

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:15.200
have done it because they
treat their APIs as products.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:17.990
They have product managers,
the product managers

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:20.450
look at who the customer
segmentation is.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.160
They look at analytics
and statistics

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.650
to see which APIs
our most popular.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:28.490
And they introduce new APIs and
new API capabilities not based

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.770
on what would be cool
but based on what's

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:35.440
actually done by what actually
the numbers they're seeing.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.640
They have a way of
becoming data driven.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.440
And this is something
that any company that

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.600
does product management
knows how to do,

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.010
but a lot of companies that
are entering into the API world

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:49.520
from sort of an enterprise
integration perspective

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.140
aren't used to
thinking that way.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.210
They're thinking about, I need
to solve a business problem.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:54.752
I need to solve a
technology problem.

00:07:54.752 --> 00:07:56.630
Wouldn't it be cool
if we could do this?

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:58.760
But the companies that
are successful with API

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:01.340
are thinking, we have
customers out there.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:04.160
Our customers are
hotel chains who

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.090
want to build integrations with
Ticketmaster's ticketing API.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:10.052
What kind of technology
platform do they have?

00:08:10.052 --> 00:08:11.510
What features are
they looking for?

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:14.030
How do we engage
with those customers

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:15.680
and make it easy to sign up?

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.792
And these companies
essentially become--

00:08:17.792 --> 00:08:20.000
they're in the same sort of
enterprise sales business

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.820
that Google Cloud is in
with managing relationships

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.400
with customers and treating
those APIs just the way

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.635
we treat any other product.

00:08:27.635 --> 00:08:30.010
So that's about all I'm going
to say about the high level

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:32.122
of APIs and API management.

00:08:32.122 --> 00:08:33.580
What we're now
going to do is Keith

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.330
is going to show us a
little bit about it.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.929
What it looks like to
be a consumer of an API?

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:41.159
And if we could
switch to the demo.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:41.770
Excellent.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:43.282
Thank you.

00:08:43.282 --> 00:08:44.490
KEITH DANEKIND: Thanks, Greg.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.070
So we heard a little bit
about what these customers are

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.477
doing when they want
to produce APIs,

00:08:49.477 --> 00:08:50.560
share them with the world.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:51.510
What does it look
like when you want

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:52.950
to consume one of these APIs?

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.170
So let's imagine you're
an application developer

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.270
and you'd like to
write an application.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.730
So I wanted to write an
application that would

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:00.990
look at historical weather.

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:03.930
I started thinking about weather
in different places and things

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:04.540
like that.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:06.210
And I thought it would be nice
to be able to go back and look

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:07.200
at historical weather.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:10.320
So the application I've
written here you can come in

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.940
and you can put in a location.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.310
In this case, I've
typed in San Francisco.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.870
When I hit this
find button up here

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:19.380
it will find weather stations
that are near that location.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.110
And for each of those
I can select one

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.402
and then I can go back and
look at the weather history.

00:09:23.402 --> 00:09:25.140
So I'm looking at San
Francisco downtown.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:26.880
I've gone back to 1930.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.920
And it's pulled down a
year of weather for me.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.910
The red line is the
high temperature

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:34.115
in Fahrenheit, blue
line low temperature.

00:09:34.115 --> 00:09:36.240
And January on the left,
and December on the right.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.190
So nice application.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:39.630
Very easy to write.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.010
How did I do this?

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:44.400
So as an application developer,
really what I'm focused on

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.110
is the user experience.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:47.970
And I just want the
data from somewhere

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:50.262
and I don't want to have to
go through a lot of trouble

00:09:50.262 --> 00:09:50.800
to get it.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:53.800
So I found this data
out on this web page.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.430
So this is an example
of a developer portal.

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:59.950
What I'm seeing here is this
company's developer experience.

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:02.348
So as an application developer
I'm going to come out here

00:10:02.348 --> 00:10:04.890
and I'm going to start reading
about what APIs are available,

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:07.150
what can I do with them
and things like that.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:08.252
So as I come into here.

00:10:08.252 --> 00:10:09.960
I'm going to look at--
looks like there's

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.412
some API products and some
things to get me started.

00:10:13.412 --> 00:10:15.870
Looks like there's some sample
code in here that might help

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:19.180
me be able to use these things.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.700
I'm going to come
down and look at--

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.680
let's try this Weather
History Try It Out--

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:26.500
provides 100 calls per
day for a developer.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:27.920
Looks great.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.140
So as I come into
here I'm going to find

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:33.200
that I've got a whole
list of APIs in here.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:36.020
I've got one that will look
up particular weather stations

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:39.600
and I've got one that'll get me
temperature data or wind data.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:42.380
The first one I want to try
is this weather station thing.

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:44.570
And they have a nice
little Try It Out over here

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.400
and some documentation.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:47.790
So I would read through this.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:49.490
And if we're thinking
like a developer

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.010
the goal is, OK, how do I
get data from this as quickly

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:52.550
as possible?

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:54.995
What kind of things
do I need to do?

00:10:54.995 --> 00:10:56.120
How do I get authenticated?

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:57.420
How do I call this?

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:01.310
So for this it looks like
there is an API key required

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:04.160
and then we're going to provide
some sort of street address

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:05.940
or place.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.220
So let's go up and register.

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:08.870
I'm already logged in here.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.370
I'm going to register
an application

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:14.010
and that gives me an API key.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.680
Let's call this Next Demo.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:23.990
OK.

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:28.530
And we're going to go
with this Try It Out here.

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.290
And then I'm going to come
up here and hit Create.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:32.980
And that's registered
my application.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:35.153
So once my application
is registered

00:11:35.153 --> 00:11:37.320
I've been given credentials
that I can try for this.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:38.892
I've got a key and a secret.

00:11:38.892 --> 00:11:40.350
I'm going to come
down here and I'm

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:44.110
going to cut and paste that key.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:47.140
Next thing I want to do is I'm
going to go back to my APIs

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:48.710
and I'm going to try this out.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:50.000
So now that I have my key.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.530
Let's go into here.

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:54.020
And let's try San
Francisco again.

00:12:03.757 --> 00:12:06.090
And that's going to pull down
some weather stations that

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:06.743
are near me.

00:12:06.743 --> 00:12:07.910
GREG BRAIL: And it's a demo.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:08.550
OK.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:09.090
KEITH DANEKIND: There they are.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:09.560
GREG BRAIL: OK.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:09.830
Never mind.

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:10.622
KEITH DANEKIND: OK.

00:12:10.622 --> 00:12:12.558
So I've got back a series
of weather stations.

00:12:12.558 --> 00:12:14.100
So there's the
latitude and longitude

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:15.540
that matched San Francisco.

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:17.730
It went out to Google Maps,
it found that latitude

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:19.175
and longitude, brought
it back, and then

00:12:19.175 --> 00:12:20.430
it's searching for
weather stations that

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:21.390
are near that point.

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:23.600
And it looks like
there's a few in there.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:24.720
And there they are.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.800
So I'm going to grab
this first one here.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.890
And then we're going
to go over to here.

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:32.932
And it looks like we can
get temperature and wind

00:12:32.932 --> 00:12:34.890
data for that particular
weather station.

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:38.958
So let's look up historical
temperature data.

00:12:38.958 --> 00:12:41.570
I'm going to grab an API key.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:48.650
And let's go back a few years.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:55.570
Let's go back to,
let's say 1950.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:56.696
And it's a demo.

00:12:56.696 --> 00:12:59.350
[LAUGHS] OK.

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:00.430
We'll go back to our key.

00:13:05.721 --> 00:13:07.645
Let's try this one.

00:13:10.563 --> 00:13:12.980
GREG BRAIL: By the way, we
will be deleting all these APIs

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:14.722
keys when--

00:13:14.722 --> 00:13:15.930
KEITH DANEKIND: Thanks, Greg.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:18.150
GREG BRAIL: Because this
is going to be on YouTube.

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:18.370
KEITH DANEKIND: OK.

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:18.830
Now it works.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:20.460
My API key is a little
bit stale there.

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:23.750
So I have got weather data back
for this particular weather

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:25.140
station that year.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:29.100
And then there's a whole array
of 365 days of weather data.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:30.350
So that was pretty easy to do.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:33.780
I could take that and I could
build an application from it.

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.867
GREG BRAIL: So now we've
impersonated an API consumer.

00:13:36.867 --> 00:13:37.700
KEITH DANEKIND: Yes.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:38.300
GREG BRAIL: Now
we're going to go

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:41.270
behind the curtains a little bit
and impersonate an API product

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:42.050
manager.

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:42.200
KEITH DANEKIND: Yes.

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:43.760
GREG BRAIL: Basically,
how would you,

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:45.950
as a company that wants
to offer such an API,

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:48.320
make the sign on experience
for your customers

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.588
as easy as what
Keith just showed?

00:13:50.588 --> 00:13:52.130
And how do you
actually, as a product

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:53.450
manager-- what do you do?

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:56.253
How do you actually keep
track of who's using your API?

00:13:56.253 --> 00:13:57.170
KEITH DANEKIND: Right.

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.030
So I've configured
some things in here

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:01.670
to productize these APIs.

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:02.630
I want to think about--

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:04.400
I've got a set of
services, but how

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:06.570
do I turn those into a
package that I can share?

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:08.570
I have different people
who want to consume them

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:10.220
and I might want to
package them different ways

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:12.560
because different people have
different rights to these.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:14.900
Let's imagine I've got a bunch
of partners, some of them

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:16.400
are allowed to do
some things, others

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:17.390
are allowed to do other things.

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:19.760
And I want to control how
frequently they can call this.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:22.200
I want to place some rate limits
on them and things like that.

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.075
So I've packaged these
up into these products

00:14:24.075 --> 00:14:25.220
that we see here.

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:28.877
What I'm really concerned
about daily though is,

00:14:28.877 --> 00:14:30.210
what do the analytics look like?

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:30.960
Who's calling them?

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:31.793
What are they doing?

00:14:31.793 --> 00:14:33.675
How can I improve
my API program?

00:14:33.675 --> 00:14:35.050
So for that, I'm
going to come in

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:37.508
and we're going to look at a
few different reports in here.

00:14:37.508 --> 00:14:39.470
So I'm now playing the
role of an API manager.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:41.450
And what I might do on
a daily basis is come in

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:43.320
and look at some
of these reports.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.470
So what I'm seeing here
is my proxy performance.

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:47.330
And I can see traffic
rolling into here

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:50.430
and I get an idea of the
most basic metric here.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:53.070
How many people are calling this
thing and what are they doing?

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:55.040
So there's traffic,
average response time,

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:58.670
traffic by proxy, average
response time by proxy.

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:03.838
Another thing I might be
interested in is looking at--

00:15:03.838 --> 00:15:05.940
Let me restart these
so my demo's better.

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:09.025
OK.

00:15:09.025 --> 00:15:10.733
Another the thing I
might want to look at

00:15:10.733 --> 00:15:14.170
is developer engagement.

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:16.330
And that's going to show
me who's called here?

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:18.440
So how many people have called
this, what have they done,

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:19.398
and that sort of thing?

00:15:21.347 --> 00:15:22.930
Let's jump ahead to
one that's loaded.

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.130
So another thing that's
really important.

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:26.210
Let's look at devices.

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:30.040
So I want to know who's on
iOS, who's on Windows, who's

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:31.930
on Android, that sort of thing.

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:35.500
This might influence how I'm
going to build my mobile apps

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:36.590
and things like that.

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:38.950
So this report is going to
show me who's coming in,

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:44.080
and what they're doing, and how
much traffic I have by device.

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:46.157
I may come in and
want to look at a map.

00:15:46.157 --> 00:15:48.740
And the map would show me where
all my traffic is coming from.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:51.250
And I can zoom in and I can
look at a particular location

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:55.150
and get an idea of who's
calling from where.

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:57.300
So I've got some traffic
coming from California.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:15:58.260
Not unexpected.

00:15:58.260 --> 00:15:59.460
But I can get an
idea of where people

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.680
are calling from the latency
from those different locations

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:02.555
and things like that.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:06.630
I might want to create
a custom report.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.130
And in this custom
report, I'm going

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:09.713
to look at traffic
by weather station.

00:16:09.713 --> 00:16:11.400
So maybe I have a
curiosity just to know

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:13.650
where are people searching
for these weather stations.

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:16.230
Which ones are interesting
and what are they pulling up?

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:19.480
So there's a number of metrics
that are provided for me.

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.120
And I've also collected
a custom metric

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:22.570
that I grabbed out
of the request,

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:23.520
and that was the
particular weather

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:24.910
stations they're looking for.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:28.172
And that's what's
shown in this report.

00:16:28.172 --> 00:16:30.630
So that's a quick tour of what
I might do as an API product

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:32.770
manager when I look through
all these different analytics.

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:33.478
GREG BRAIL: Cool.

00:16:33.478 --> 00:16:35.460
Thanks.

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:37.740
We can go back to
the slides now.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:39.270
We've talked a
little bit about what

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:42.750
it would look like to be
the consumer of an API.

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:45.085
Basically, what we
saw was, gee, it

00:16:45.085 --> 00:16:46.710
was really easy to
sign up to get a key

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:49.032
to get credentials and
start calling that API.

00:16:49.032 --> 00:16:50.490
We also looked a
little bit at what

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:53.040
it would look like to be
an API product manager.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:55.710
If I'm an API product
manager what do I do?

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:58.318
How do I actually manage the
people who were using API?

00:16:58.318 --> 00:17:00.360
And that's really important
because the companies

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:04.200
that have succeeded with APIs
have treated them as products.

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:07.619
And really taken that product
management discipline to heart.

00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:10.313
We're going to get back to
some of the technology later.

00:17:10.313 --> 00:17:11.980
But we're going to
move on a little bit,

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:13.950
and we're going to talk
a little bit about some

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:15.540
of the high level
technologies you

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:19.690
need to understand in order to
run a successful API program.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:21.329
What are some
specific things that

00:17:21.329 --> 00:17:26.579
make APIs and API management
a different kind of technology

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:28.523
than other things
that are out there?

00:17:28.523 --> 00:17:29.940
Well, the first
thing is what I've

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:31.920
been calling a lot API
consumer management,

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.020
but it's really API management.

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:37.110
API management is
a set of technology

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.900
that manages the relationship
between the consumer's

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:43.290
of an API and the
producers of an API.

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:46.650
And it's really the only bit
of technology in the enterprise

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:47.940
that does that.

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:50.670
It's extremely important
for these kinds of use cases

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.180
we've been talking about.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:54.600
Because it's the only way
for an API product manager

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:57.450
to understand who
is using their API,

00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:59.520
how much are they
using their API.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:01.650
And allows them
to create products

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:04.740
that actually comprise
the APIs that allow you

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:06.360
to have different offerings.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:10.170
So in Keith's demo, there
was a free developer version

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:13.980
of the weather app that you
could call 100 times a day.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:16.170
And presumably, if we
had clicked father maybe

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.630
if he wanted to launch
an app on the app store

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:22.470
and have 100 million API
calls a day on that same API,

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:24.570
he would probably have
some way on that form

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:26.400
to speak to the
API product manager

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:30.810
directly and negotiate terms of
service or prices or whatever.

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:32.610
Sophisticated
companies and companies

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:36.330
that have large numbers of
APIs inside their companies

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:38.670
use the API management
product not only

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:41.100
to manage relationships
with external partners

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:42.810
but also internally.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:44.790
If you want to be
able to, say, offer

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:47.970
an API within a large
financial services organization

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:51.100
and let anybody in the
organization use your API,

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:54.280
you need a way for them to
sign up, get credentials.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:57.210
You may have APIs that you
want to make visible only

00:18:57.210 --> 00:18:58.780
to certain teams.

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:01.530
You may eventually want to make
those APIs visible externally

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:04.590
and you may have certain
partners who have access

00:19:04.590 --> 00:19:07.278
to certain API and certain
partners that don't.

00:19:07.278 --> 00:19:08.820
Many companies that
have been running

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:10.890
APIs for many
years in production

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:13.710
need these kind of capabilities
and rather than build

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:16.320
a lot of these things
ad hoc and from scratch

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:21.180
as some companies do, API
management products actually

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:25.120
exist that basically give you
a way to manage all this stuff.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:28.080
So really when I talk about
APIs and API management,

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.630
I spend a lot of time on
this because to me, this

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.610
is what API management is.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:35.760
It's technology to manage that
relationship between producers

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:37.028
and consumers.

00:19:37.028 --> 00:19:38.820
But there are other
parts to API management

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:41.040
as well that are really
important in that in order

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:45.150
to run a successful API,
it's important to understand.

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:46.830
One of them is OAuth.

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:49.800
OAuth as the de facto
standard for access control

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:51.540
for mobile
applications and really

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:53.160
any kind of applications.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:56.080
And it's even used for your
single sign on via web apps.

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:58.960
If you're using an app
that uses a Google API,

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:00.510
you're using OAuth.

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:03.240
If you're using a website
that uses these APIs,

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:06.390
or if you're signing into
Twitter, or if you're at work

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:09.150
and you clicked on your
internal link that signs you

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:11.070
into your health
insurance account

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:15.860
and the web browser redirects
47 times, that's also OAuth.

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:18.090
OAuth has become this
de facto standard

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:20.830
and also it's a set
of IETF standards,

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:23.490
because it makes possible to
decouple the different parts

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:25.740
of the identity relationship.

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:28.440
It allows the API
gateway, which is usually

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.810
a proxy that's sitting
in between the clients

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:34.500
and the back ends, to
actually look at access tokens

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:38.440
and decide whether an API call
should be accepted or rejected.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:40.650
And it allows that API
gateway to be separate

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:44.770
from the identity system that
actually signs in the user.

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:46.620
So that's why when
you see websites

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:49.410
that have Sign In via
Google, Sign In via Facebook,

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:53.550
or Sign In via GitHub all
of that is done using OAuth.

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:56.880
And people build open APIs
and mobile applications

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.980
using that same technology.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:01.050
And an interesting
thing about OAuth

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:04.260
is that each OAuth
token, which is the thing

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:06.240
that your mobile
device actually sends

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:08.970
when it makes an API
call to identify you,

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:10.300
not only is unique.

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:12.990
So it would tell you it
identifies a certain device,

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:15.390
it identifies the
user who signed

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:18.060
in using the IDP in
order to get the token.

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.250
But it also identifies
the application

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:21.990
that made the request.

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.690
So the reason why OAuth is so
important to API management

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:26.290
is going back to
this slide here.

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:29.850
Because each OAuth token
identifies the consumer

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:33.900
of the API exactly what
application built the API.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:35.220
This can be really important.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:39.450
We've seen cases for instance,
where a customer launches

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:42.510
an API and they have a
problem in production

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:45.540
because a new version
of a client application

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:50.190
has gone out of control
for any kind of numbers

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:53.730
and is sending thousands of
requests a second to the API

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:55.740
from phones all over the world.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:57.750
Well, without
understanding exactly

00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:00.240
which application is
making that request

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:02.380
there's not much
you can do about it.

00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:04.300
But if you have an API
management system in,

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:05.992
or it's wire it
up to your OAuth,

00:22:05.992 --> 00:22:07.950
you might be able to say,
take all the requests

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:11.160
from this application and black
hole them to another server.

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:14.550
Or even reject them entirely
at the API gateway layer

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:18.670
so they don't bring down the
rest of my infrastructure.

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:20.920
Two other really important
bits of API management.

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:22.420
One of them are quotas.

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:25.960
Quotas are something we
see in every API today.

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:27.820
Those of you who are
Google Cloud customers,

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:29.945
and we hope you will all
be Google Cloud customers,

00:22:29.945 --> 00:22:33.320
are probably frustrated with
the fact that quotas exist.

00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:36.580
But they're essential for any
company that offers a platform.

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:39.070
Google has quotas
on its Cloud APIs

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:42.280
to prevent for instance, someone
putting their credentials

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:45.130
in GitHub and getting
picked up there and used

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:49.000
to spin the 100,000 virtual
machines for Bitcoin mining.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:52.720
I would say that never happens,
but it totally happens.

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:55.240
Similarly, we have
quotas to protect us

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:56.950
against those sorts of things.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:59.098
Anyone who has an API in
production you need quotas

00:22:59.098 --> 00:23:00.640
if you're going to
let people sign up

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:03.070
for your API for self-service.

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:04.330
Why do you want to do that?

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:07.510
You want to do that
because that's self-service

00:23:07.510 --> 00:23:09.850
that we saw, any developer
can click a button

00:23:09.850 --> 00:23:11.920
and use an API
within five minutes,

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:13.450
it only works if
you have some way

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:16.720
to control the amount of
traffic coming into your API

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:19.960
so that a new user doesn't
overwhelm your capacity.

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:22.810
And you can't do that
unless you have a quota

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:25.090
and unless you have an
API management system that

00:23:25.090 --> 00:23:28.150
can let you control which
application is using your API

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:29.410
and by how much.

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:31.540
Good quota systems let you
set all kinds of quotas

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:33.130
and also let you do
things like reset

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:35.020
them, have different
levels of service

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:36.860
for different customers,
and change them.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:40.990
So for instance, if you actually
do need 100,000 VMs on Google

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:43.630
Cloud, there is a way that
you can ask a Google support

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:46.180
person to increase your
quota because now we

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:48.430
know who you are, we trust
you, and we know you're not

00:23:48.430 --> 00:23:49.510
doing bitcoin mining.

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:51.550
So these are the
kinds of things that

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:55.450
become really important
when you run API at scale.

00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:57.570
Security threats are
a big deal of course.

00:23:57.570 --> 00:24:00.983
OAuth is a big part of
security but API gateways

00:24:00.983 --> 00:24:02.650
are very important
for security but they

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:04.720
operate at the highest level.

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:07.300
They're able to tell
you if an API call has

00:24:07.300 --> 00:24:10.120
a legitimate access
token or it comes

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:12.440
from a legitimate application.

00:24:12.440 --> 00:24:14.720
But there are other kinds
of security threats as well.

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:16.303
For instance, let's
imagine that Keith

00:24:16.303 --> 00:24:20.860
had amended his API to let you
send in using some sort of JSON

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:23.230
payload what the weather is now.

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:25.480
Hey, you know, it's actually
raining in San Francisco.

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:27.712
Your app told me it was sunny.

00:24:27.712 --> 00:24:29.920
Well, a whole lot of things
could go wrong with that.

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:30.565
Right?

00:24:30.565 --> 00:24:32.440
To start with, the
description of the weather

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:34.440
might not be something
that they want to publish

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:36.590
but that's a different problem.

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:39.430
But yeah, you're accepting
JSON from untrusted people

00:24:39.430 --> 00:24:40.330
on the internet.

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:43.600
So what's to stop someone from
sending you a 100 megabyte

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:44.890
comment.

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:47.860
Not that JSON has comments,
but a 100 megabyte string

00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:50.920
or something that's less
than 10,000 levels deep.

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:52.870
Or instead of your
weather station ID

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:56.770
they put in semicolon
delete star from customers

00:24:56.770 --> 00:24:58.370
or something like that.

00:24:58.370 --> 00:25:00.380
All of these kinds
of threats are real

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:04.150
and can happen when you're
accepting data like JSON or XML

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:06.130
or something else from
an untrusted party.

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:07.630
And it's really
important to be able

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:09.110
protect against those things.

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:11.980
So a lot of APIs gateways
can do that as well.

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:13.240
I'll also mention DDoS.

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:16.660
A lot of people say, Oh, I need
an API gateway they protect me

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:18.700
from DDoS attacks.

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:21.520
The truth is that API
gateway technology

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:24.610
is essential for these kinds
of things because of quotas,

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:26.680
because of
authentication, but that's

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:28.510
got to be a fully
multi-layered approach.

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:30.610
So that's why we have
DDoS protection built

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:31.900
into Google Cloud.

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:34.630
We have additional products like
Cloud Armor that give you more

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:36.820
sophisticated DDoS protection.

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:39.700
But there are many layers
necessary in any kind

00:25:39.700 --> 00:25:44.850
of internet facing products
to protect against those.

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:46.850
Finally, I'll mention one
part of API management

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:49.730
that's not really as tightly
connected to the others

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:51.620
but it's something
that becomes very

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:53.150
important in the
real world, which

00:25:53.150 --> 00:25:56.450
is mediation and
transformation and integration.

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:58.370
Many of the examples I
used at the beginning

00:25:58.370 --> 00:26:00.350
of this presentation
were companies

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:02.570
that created new
digital experiences

00:26:02.570 --> 00:26:04.152
on top of legacy systems.

00:26:04.152 --> 00:26:06.110
Because almost everybody
out there in the world

00:26:06.110 --> 00:26:07.880
has legacy systems.

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:10.070
Well, those legacy
systems usually

00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:13.700
don't present the kind of API
that for instance, the Google

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:17.240
Home device expects or
that a mobile application

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:18.200
developer is using.

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:21.920
They may return
you know 25k of XML

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:26.390
when what a mobile app really
needs is 200 bytes and JSON.

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:29.180
They may not have been
designed for the internet era

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:31.070
and may not even
contemplate being

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:34.010
used by hundreds of thousands
or millions of people.

00:26:34.010 --> 00:26:36.600
Their response time may be low.

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.240
So it can be very,
very important

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:41.120
to be able to use something
like an API gateway

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:43.440
to mediate and transform
different technologies

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:45.680
so you can take that
SOAP application that's

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:48.950
been in production for 15
years, build an API on it,

00:26:48.950 --> 00:26:50.450
and then be able
to use it to launch

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:52.160
a new digital experience.

00:26:52.160 --> 00:26:54.170
This even goes sort of
in the forward direction.

00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:57.320
Developers who are building
things with microservices today

00:26:57.320 --> 00:26:59.420
love to use
technology like gRPC.

00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:01.730
It's efficient, it's
easily integrated

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:04.160
with certain
programming languages.

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:08.180
It is easier to develop
depending on the context.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:09.680
But often, if you're
doing something

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:11.510
like talking to a
mobile app you want

00:27:11.510 --> 00:27:15.050
to transform that into something
like JSON over HTTP, which

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:17.030
is something that all
developers can understand

00:27:17.030 --> 00:27:21.600
and an increasing number of
APIs gateways do that as well.

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:23.000
So let me take a
few minutes here

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:25.040
and talk about our products.

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:27.800
Because so far, if I'm talking
about API Management, API

00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:31.490
Management companies who
actually do APIs, and what

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:35.000
it can do in particular,
sorry, in a generic way,

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:38.030
let me talk about what we
have to offer at Google.

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:40.220
Google actually
offers two products

00:27:40.220 --> 00:27:42.200
in the world of API management.

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:44.420
These are both actually
owned by the same team

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:47.570
and I'm responsible for the
architecture of both of them.

00:27:47.570 --> 00:27:50.750
Cloud Endpoints has been a part
of Google Cloud for a while.

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:52.580
The advantage of
Cloud Endpoints is

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:57.030
that it is a very
small, simple, fast,

00:27:57.030 --> 00:27:58.730
easy to set up
product that gives you

00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:00.830
the basics of API management.

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:05.580
You can verify an API
key, you can set a quota.

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:07.520
You can do some
basic authentication.

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:10.040
A lot of companies use
Cloud Endpoints in order

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:13.100
to build digital experiences
in front of workloads that

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:14.960
already run on Google Cloud.

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:17.480
It takes advantage of many
of the API capabilities

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.910
that are built into Google
Cloud, such as the quotas

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:22.130
and authentication you're
already using if you're

00:28:22.130 --> 00:28:24.630
a Google Cloud customer.

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:26.700
The other product
we have is Apigee.

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:29.940
Apigee was acquired by
Google in the late 2016.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:32.640
It's what I've primarily been
working on for the last third

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:34.150
of my career.

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:38.010
And then Apigee does
basically everything

00:28:38.010 --> 00:28:39.150
around API management.

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:41.160
Not only running as
a basic API Gateway

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:42.990
to do things like
authentication,

00:28:42.990 --> 00:28:46.260
but also there's things like a
customizable developer portal,

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:49.560
a highly sophisticated and
customizable quota mechanism,

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:54.540
caching, API monitoring,
customizable API analytics,

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:55.960
monetization.

00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:59.190
So if you choose to
charge money for an API

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:03.060
you can use Apigee to assign a
value to particular API calls

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:05.850
and then generate a bill
for customers later.

00:29:05.850 --> 00:29:08.190
And many companies,
especially larger enterprises

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:11.970
that have a lot of these
enterprise complexities to them

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:14.190
find that they need the
sophistication of an API

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:16.620
management product
like Apigee in order

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:18.138
to really solve their problems.

00:29:18.138 --> 00:29:19.680
So the ones who were
saying, hey, you

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:21.597
know the foreign exchange
department shouldn't

00:29:21.597 --> 00:29:25.230
be able to call the equity
trading departments APIs

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:27.210
unless it's approved by a VP.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:30.180
These are the kinds of things
that happen in the real world

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:32.880
and they make API management
more than just checking

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:35.490
an API key in a Redis
table but actually

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:38.820
doing some more sophisticated
roles based access controls.

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:41.400
These are the kinds of things
that a lot of customers

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:44.650
use Apigee to do.

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:47.340
It's important to mention
that Apigee actually

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:48.490
runs in multiple modes.

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:50.370
One of them is
software as a service.

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:53.670
Everything runs by Google in the
cloud in all of Google's data

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:54.720
centers.

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:56.430
Other end of the
spectrum is on premise.

00:29:56.430 --> 00:29:59.055
You can take the whole thing and
put it in your own data center

00:29:59.055 --> 00:30:01.110
and not touch the
internet at all.

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:03.720
There's a number of companies
who have historically

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:06.010
had to do that for a
whole number of reasons.

00:30:06.010 --> 00:30:08.907
What we're seeing now
even more and more

00:30:08.907 --> 00:30:10.740
people need to be able
to run in multi cloud

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:12.302
and in a hybrid environment.

00:30:12.302 --> 00:30:14.760
And obviously, you heard a lot
about that at the conference

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:16.020
already today.

00:30:16.020 --> 00:30:19.440
So Apigee also has a hybrid
mode where the API gateway that

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:22.230
actually sits in between the
client and server of the API

00:30:22.230 --> 00:30:26.250
traffic can run on your own
data center, or your own cloud,

00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:28.095
or really anywhere
you want it to be.

00:30:28.095 --> 00:30:29.970
While some of the other
parts of Apigee like,

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:31.890
the management
and the analytics,

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:35.407
can run as a service run by us.

00:30:35.407 --> 00:30:36.990
And with that said,
Keith is now going

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:41.250
to show us how to actually
build the APIs that he showed

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:43.440
and apply the API
management capabilities he

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:45.090
showed using Apigee.

00:30:47.775 --> 00:30:49.150
And sorry, can we
go to the demo?

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:52.960
There we go.

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:56.670
KEITH DANEKIND: So we talked
about how people consume this.

00:30:56.670 --> 00:30:58.960
But what did it take
to build this service?

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:01.645
So we saw that we have this
nice weather application

00:31:01.645 --> 00:31:02.770
with all this data on here.

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:05.590
Where's the data coming
from and what's behind this?

00:31:05.590 --> 00:31:10.630
So let me talk about how
I built that using Apigee.

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:13.660
So what we have in here
is on the left hand side

00:31:13.660 --> 00:31:15.730
there you see a
client application

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:19.840
and it uses API keys to
talk to the back end system.

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:23.770
The data is out in BigQuery and
I've got one of the public data

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:25.450
sets out there is
historical weather

00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:27.552
and it has weather going
back a few hundred years.

00:31:27.552 --> 00:31:29.510
And I thought it'd be
kind of cool to use that.

00:31:29.510 --> 00:31:31.510
But the challenges
I ran into was

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:33.400
I need to authenticate
with Google Cloud

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:35.980
and to do that I have to
go get an access token.

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:38.650
There's some credentials that
need to be exchanged in there

00:31:38.650 --> 00:31:41.450
and I need to send a
header to the back end.

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:43.670
So that process isn't
really important to who

00:31:43.670 --> 00:31:44.590
is consuming this.

00:31:44.590 --> 00:31:45.970
On the consumption
side of this I

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:47.980
wanted to have API keys
so I could identify

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:49.350
different applications.

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:51.110
I could on board
people pretty quickly.

00:31:51.110 --> 00:31:53.650
So what we're seeing in here
is the left side of this

00:31:53.650 --> 00:31:55.450
is one set of security,
the right side

00:31:55.450 --> 00:31:56.873
is a different set of security.

00:31:56.873 --> 00:31:58.540
I also had some other
concerns in there.

00:31:58.540 --> 00:31:59.560
Some of the things
that Greg mentioned.

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:00.760
Some of the things
we worry about

00:32:00.760 --> 00:32:02.580
as we start to share
data with new people.

00:32:02.580 --> 00:32:04.330
And those are things
like, how do we build

00:32:04.330 --> 00:32:05.413
a facade on this back end?

00:32:05.413 --> 00:32:06.872
So I want to take
everything that's

00:32:06.872 --> 00:32:08.590
back there no matter
when it was written

00:32:08.590 --> 00:32:11.158
and make it look clean and
new to the consumers of this.

00:32:11.158 --> 00:32:13.450
So I want to change the
requests and responses that are

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:15.340
coming back and forth in there.

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:17.350
I wanted to control
access to some features.

00:32:17.350 --> 00:32:20.060
So you can try it out for free
and you can get 100 calls.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:22.060
If you're a valued partner
maybe you can come in

00:32:22.060 --> 00:32:23.643
and you can look at
more things if you

00:32:23.643 --> 00:32:25.570
have a different
relationship with us.

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:27.580
I wanted to rate limit
to protect the back.

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:29.420
So we need to have
things in there

00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:31.960
so that if your application
has a loop in there that's

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:33.430
uncontrolled, we
don't want to melt

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:34.540
the back end for everybody.

00:32:34.540 --> 00:32:35.582
We want to shut you down.

00:32:35.582 --> 00:32:36.580
So rate limiting.

00:32:36.580 --> 00:32:38.830
And I want to impose some
quotas for business reasons.

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:41.413
I want to have different tiers
of service for different people

00:32:41.413 --> 00:32:43.390
so that I could
charge accordingly.

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:44.870
And I want to add
caching in here.

00:32:44.870 --> 00:32:46.510
So if somebody makes the
same request twice let's

00:32:46.510 --> 00:32:48.093
just call it from
memory and let's not

00:32:48.093 --> 00:32:49.810
go all the way to the back end.

00:32:49.810 --> 00:32:51.560
And reformat the
request in the response.

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:54.060
So that's what I built. That's
what this diagram represents.

00:32:54.060 --> 00:32:56.570
Let's look at
what's behind this.

00:32:56.570 --> 00:32:58.340
So how did I get there?

00:32:58.340 --> 00:32:59.840
So the next role
for me to play here

00:32:59.840 --> 00:33:01.500
as the demo person
is we're going

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:04.910
to look at what does it
take to build and manage

00:33:04.910 --> 00:33:07.530
a service that you want
to expose to other people.

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:09.650
So I started off
with a specification

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:12.135
and I thought about, I
know what's in the database

00:33:12.135 --> 00:33:13.760
and know how that's
formatted, but what

00:33:13.760 --> 00:33:15.930
do I want to present
to the outside world?

00:33:15.930 --> 00:33:18.410
And for that I wrote this
spec an OpenAPI spec.

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:19.460
I did this in Apigee.

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:20.960
I've come in here
and I've described

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:22.070
what the endpoints are.

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:24.450
I've described the data
formats and things like that.

00:33:24.450 --> 00:33:26.185
I did that in this
left hand pane.

00:33:26.185 --> 00:33:27.560
On the right hand
side here I can

00:33:27.560 --> 00:33:30.060
see a rendering of what my
documentation will look like.

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:32.600
So that was the documentation
we saw on the website.

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:35.690
This is what guided the
developers on how to use it.

00:33:35.690 --> 00:33:37.960
The next thing I did
was I took that spec

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:40.690
and I turned it into
an API proxy in Apigee.

00:33:40.690 --> 00:33:42.150
And what this does
is this is going

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:43.870
to control that
request and response

00:33:43.870 --> 00:33:45.600
flow that goes back and forth.

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:47.100
What we see in here
is there's a URL

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:49.480
appear I'm going to start
calling Inside of Apigee.

00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:54.970
And that's going to call this
BigQuery API on the back end.

00:33:54.970 --> 00:33:57.880
If I click over here on Develop
what I'm going to see in here

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:01.070
is a graphical depiction of
the request and response flow.

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:03.940
So what we're seeing is this
is the request coming in

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:06.130
on the top, right
up here, and then we

00:34:06.130 --> 00:34:07.940
see the response coming
back on the bottom.

00:34:07.940 --> 00:34:09.773
And I going to do a few
things along the way

00:34:09.773 --> 00:34:12.280
to manage this request
and response flow.

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:13.989
For example, I'm
going to come in

00:34:13.989 --> 00:34:15.469
and I will do some
rate limiting.

00:34:15.469 --> 00:34:17.590
And right down here,
I'm going to limit them

00:34:17.590 --> 00:34:18.725
to 10 calls per minute.

00:34:18.725 --> 00:34:20.350
That's not very fast
but it's something

00:34:20.350 --> 00:34:23.650
I can demonstrate
breaking in a demo.

00:34:23.650 --> 00:34:26.170
So these different policies
are run on the request side.

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:28.210
These policies are run
on the response side.

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:30.810
I have different flows in here
that can do different things.

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:32.560
I've got a whole set
of policies over here

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:35.210
that when I click that plus
button they'll get added.

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:36.503
Things for traffic management.

00:34:36.503 --> 00:34:38.920
How do we control just the
basic flow going back and forth

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:39.670
in there?

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:41.050
Things for security.

00:34:41.050 --> 00:34:42.728
As we start to share
with new people

00:34:42.728 --> 00:34:44.270
were always concerned
about security.

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:47.949
So basic auth to check for
user names and passwords.

00:34:47.949 --> 00:34:50.679
OAuth, as Greg mentioned, to
tie into our identity management

00:34:50.679 --> 00:34:53.920
system and to identify
who's calling.

00:34:53.920 --> 00:34:55.540
I might want to reformat things.

00:34:55.540 --> 00:34:57.340
I might want to turn
my JSON and XML,

00:34:57.340 --> 00:35:00.930
XML to JSON if I've got a
legacy system on the back end.

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:03.460
I may want to extend this with
Java call outs, Python call

00:35:03.460 --> 00:35:04.293
outs, or JavaScript.

00:35:04.293 --> 00:35:05.668
Or I might want
to take advantage

00:35:05.668 --> 00:35:07.120
of certain things
in Google Cloud

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:09.965
by using some of these
extensions that are specific.

00:35:09.965 --> 00:35:12.340
So those are all different
policies I might add in there.

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:15.135
That manages my request
and my response flow.

00:35:15.135 --> 00:35:17.260
And then depending on how
this is called, depending

00:35:17.260 --> 00:35:18.968
on what people put at
the end of the URL,

00:35:18.968 --> 00:35:20.660
I might want to do
different things.

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:23.320
So looking for a weather station
I might execute these things.

00:35:23.320 --> 00:35:24.820
First thing I'm
going to do in there

00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:27.490
is look up an API key that's
going to give me access

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:28.667
to Google Maps.

00:35:28.667 --> 00:35:31.000
And I'm going to use that to
make a call out and look up

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:31.700
that city.

00:35:31.700 --> 00:35:34.200
So when I type in San Francisco,
what I'm really looking for

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:36.310
is the latitude and
longitude of San Francisco

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:39.130
because that's how the weather
stations are in my database.

00:35:39.130 --> 00:35:40.880
I'm going to look at
the response of that.

00:35:40.880 --> 00:35:42.890
I'm going to grab the latitude
and longitude out of that.

00:35:42.890 --> 00:35:45.670
I'm going to draw nice little
box around that with some code

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:47.588
and figure out an
area where I want

00:35:47.588 --> 00:35:48.880
to search for weather stations.

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:50.180
That's right there.

00:35:50.180 --> 00:35:54.160
And then finally, I'm going
to build up a BigQuery request

00:35:54.160 --> 00:35:56.890
and I'm going to
completely replace

00:35:56.890 --> 00:36:00.370
this request with a whole
new body and send that in.

00:36:00.370 --> 00:36:02.875
And this is completely
hidden from my consumer.

00:36:02.875 --> 00:36:04.250
When I get the
response back, I'm

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:06.130
going to format the
response that comes back

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:08.560
to make it look more
pleasing and more

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:09.975
useful to my developer.

00:36:09.975 --> 00:36:11.350
So those are all
different things

00:36:11.350 --> 00:36:13.490
that I would do in
here to build this up.

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:15.080
So I'm going to
send in requests,

00:36:15.080 --> 00:36:17.940
I'm going to see responses.

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:22.490
Now, along the way,
it's remotely possible

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:25.762
that I made a mistake in my
code and I need to debug this.

00:36:25.762 --> 00:36:28.220
So I'm going to come in and
I'm going to show what it looks

00:36:28.220 --> 00:36:29.243
like to debug in here.

00:36:29.243 --> 00:36:31.160
What I've done is I've
turned on Trace in here

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:34.490
and this is going to let me look
at detailed request response

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:35.690
flow in here.

00:36:35.690 --> 00:36:38.210
So I'll come back over to my
weather application and we'll

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:39.680
go back to--

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:41.725
let's go back just to 2015--

00:36:41.725 --> 00:36:43.100
and we'll send a
request in there

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:46.953
to look up temperature data
and I'll send in a few.

00:36:46.953 --> 00:36:48.620
And this should shut
me down after a bit

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:50.710
because I'm being rate limited.

00:36:50.710 --> 00:36:53.160
So if it come back over to
here we'll see those rolling in

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:55.260
and we can see a detailed
response of exactly

00:36:55.260 --> 00:36:56.950
what happened in there.

00:36:56.950 --> 00:36:58.883
So here's the request coming in.

00:36:58.883 --> 00:37:00.300
We can see all the
details of what

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:01.950
was received by the client.

00:37:01.950 --> 00:37:04.650
We can see API keys
being processed,

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:07.470
metadata being loaded to
describe who the person is.

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:09.850
We see our rate limit
in here coming in.

00:37:09.850 --> 00:37:12.275
You'll notice over here
that we see a 429 error.

00:37:12.275 --> 00:37:14.400
We've applied rate limiting
because this person was

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:15.302
calling too quickly.

00:37:15.302 --> 00:37:17.010
So what they're going
to see in that case

00:37:17.010 --> 00:37:20.010
is they're going to see
a nice error that says,

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:22.080
Call rate exceeded-- this
application is limited

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:23.498
to 10 requests per minute.

00:37:23.498 --> 00:37:26.040
So instead of seeing that big
500 something horrible happened

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:27.630
and we don't know
what it is, you're

00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:29.130
going to see a
helpful error that's

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:31.630
going to help you
debug your application.

00:37:31.630 --> 00:37:33.230
So that's what I
did to build this.

00:37:33.230 --> 00:37:34.470
This was easy for me.

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:35.755
I had a back end system.

00:37:35.755 --> 00:37:37.380
I made a few calls
to it, I figured out

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:38.595
what the format was.

00:37:38.595 --> 00:37:40.470
I thought about how do
I want to manage that.

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:43.770
I went into Apigee and
here I built an API proxy

00:37:43.770 --> 00:37:46.437
and then that's how people
are going to call this.

00:37:46.437 --> 00:37:49.020
So that's a quick example of how
you build something in Apigee

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:52.562
and exactly what the
steps are to do that.

00:37:52.562 --> 00:37:53.270
GREG BRAIL: Cool.

00:37:53.270 --> 00:37:55.550
So in the beginning,
we showed you

00:37:55.550 --> 00:37:58.273
what it might look like to
be the consumer of an API

00:37:58.273 --> 00:37:59.690
then we talked a
little about what

00:37:59.690 --> 00:38:03.038
it would look like to be
an API product manager.

00:38:03.038 --> 00:38:04.580
And now Keith is
showing us what does

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:07.460
it look like to actually
use Apigee to create

00:38:07.460 --> 00:38:10.100
the policies in order to
make the API work the way

00:38:10.100 --> 00:38:11.210
that I want it to.

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:15.320
Now, if we could go back
to the slides for a sec?

00:38:15.320 --> 00:38:19.190
Something we didn't mention at
