00:41:43.490 --> 00:41:44.800
on the Dory here I can take.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:44.980
GREG BRAIL: Yeah.

00:41:44.980 --> 00:41:46.310
Well, we're not
going to present them

00:41:46.310 --> 00:41:48.050
because we don't want
to out people's names

00:41:48.050 --> 00:41:49.092
but we're going to start.

00:41:49.092 --> 00:41:51.330
And Yeah, well, let's answer
a few of the questions.

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:52.220
KEITH DANEKIND: The
first question here.

00:41:52.220 --> 00:41:53.673
How do you manage API versions?

00:41:53.673 --> 00:41:55.340
So this one comes up
almost every time I

00:41:55.340 --> 00:41:56.210
demoed this product.

00:41:56.210 --> 00:41:58.470
So there's two concepts here.

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:01.100
One, is basic revisions
in the API proxy.

00:42:01.100 --> 00:42:02.810
So let's say add
caching but I'm not

00:42:02.810 --> 00:42:05.580
changing the contract of how
this calls back and forth.

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:07.850
There is a built in revisioning
system within Apigee

00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:09.740
to manage the
revisions of those API

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:11.660
proxies you saw me manipulating.

00:42:11.660 --> 00:42:12.950
The other thing is versioning.

00:42:12.950 --> 00:42:15.000
Let's say I break
that contract and I'm

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:17.750
going to change the format
that comes back completely.

00:42:17.750 --> 00:42:19.370
There are many ways to do this.

00:42:19.370 --> 00:42:21.540
Apigee supports all
kinds of ways to do this.

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:23.630
The most common we're
seeing is that people

00:42:23.630 --> 00:42:26.540
will put V1 or V2
or V3 in the URL

00:42:26.540 --> 00:42:28.830
as far to the left as possible.

00:42:28.830 --> 00:42:30.383
And you can deploy
this API proxy,

00:42:30.383 --> 00:42:32.300
you can make a copy of
it and deploy a new one

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:35.270
and call that version 2 and
it can behave differently.

00:42:35.270 --> 00:42:36.710
You can tie the
analytics to that

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:39.403
so you can measure who's on
version 1, who's on version 2.

00:42:39.403 --> 00:42:40.820
Much better than
the old way which

00:42:40.820 --> 00:42:43.440
is just to turn off one
and see who gets angry.

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:44.190
So this is better.

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:45.732
GREG BRAIL: And you
can also find out

00:42:45.732 --> 00:42:47.840
who is using that old
version of the API so--

00:42:47.840 --> 00:42:48.110
KEITH DANEKIND: Yes.

00:42:48.110 --> 00:42:50.210
GREG BRAIL: --you can maybe
even send them an email and say,

00:42:50.210 --> 00:42:51.020
what's going on?

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:52.080
KEITH DANEKIND:
Or slow them down.

00:42:52.080 --> 00:42:53.100
GREG BRAIL: [LAUGHS]
Or slow them--

00:42:53.100 --> 00:42:54.630
KEITH DANEKIND: Give
them A little motivation.

00:42:54.630 --> 00:42:55.330
GREG BRAIL: You
could do that, yes.

00:42:55.330 --> 00:42:57.300
KEITH DANEKIND: So that's how
we would do versioning in here.

00:42:57.300 --> 00:42:58.800
And you can keep
iterating on there,

00:42:58.800 --> 00:43:00.870
you can measure it,
and do it formally.

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:02.640
So nice feature of that.

00:43:02.640 --> 00:43:04.830
Let me see what else is in here.

00:43:04.830 --> 00:43:06.660
GREG BRAIL: Someone
asked about open API 3.

00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:08.310
I actually thought
that we either

00:43:08.310 --> 00:43:10.678
did that we're about to do it.

00:43:10.678 --> 00:43:12.220
KEITH DANEKIND: It
is on the roadmap.

00:43:12.220 --> 00:43:13.678
I don't know if we
can give details

00:43:13.678 --> 00:43:15.330
since they record
us and everything,

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:17.445
but it's on the roadmap.

00:43:17.445 --> 00:43:18.070
GREG BRAIL: OK.

00:43:18.070 --> 00:43:18.780
Let's see.

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:20.690
Why would anyone
use Cloud Endpoints?

00:43:20.690 --> 00:43:23.310
I really should be I'll
answer the question by now.

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:24.870
One of the advantage
about endpoints

00:43:24.870 --> 00:43:26.912
is it's very simple to
get started with it's also

00:43:26.912 --> 00:43:28.720
very inexpensive.

00:43:28.720 --> 00:43:30.740
So it does a few things,
but it does them well,

00:43:30.740 --> 00:43:32.860
and it does them
at a very low cost.

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:34.590
This can be very
important if you

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:37.470
have-- because what we see
for instance, is companies

00:43:37.470 --> 00:43:40.530
have very sophisticated API
programs with lots of bells

00:43:40.530 --> 00:43:43.440
and whistles and complex
transformations and things,

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:45.840
but that may be for
a low volume things.

00:43:45.840 --> 00:43:47.670
For a consumer
application that's

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:51.730
called hundreds of millions
or billions of times a day,

00:43:51.730 --> 00:43:54.260
sometimes you may only need
a basic quota and an API

00:43:54.260 --> 00:43:54.760
key check.

00:43:54.760 --> 00:43:56.920
And Cloud Endpoints can do that.

00:43:56.920 --> 00:43:58.920
And it can do that
at a low price point.

00:43:58.920 --> 00:44:03.202
And we're actually continuing
to develop Cloud Endpoints.

00:44:03.202 --> 00:44:05.160
We're going to see
actually, over the next year

00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:06.960
or two some more
enhancements to it.

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:09.030
And over time Cloud
Endpoints and Apigee

00:44:09.030 --> 00:44:14.220
will be happy together.

00:44:14.220 --> 00:44:17.690
Sorry, my app crashed.

00:44:17.690 --> 00:44:18.190
OK.

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:19.890
Is there are another
one you want to take?

00:44:19.890 --> 00:44:21.265
KEITH DANEKIND:
I see another one

00:44:21.265 --> 00:44:23.280
in here asking about
GraphQL API support.

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:24.947
So this is coming up
more and more where

00:44:24.947 --> 00:44:27.550
people are asking about this.

00:44:27.550 --> 00:44:30.960
You can send anything that's
HTTP back and forth in here.

00:44:30.960 --> 00:44:32.730
It doesn't necessarily
have to be REST.

00:44:32.730 --> 00:44:34.950
So people use this with SOAP.

00:44:34.950 --> 00:44:37.530
I've seen people use it with
OData and other protocols

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:39.030
they're going to
add on top of that.

00:44:39.030 --> 00:44:40.820
We're looking at how
to support GraphQL.

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:42.630
We don't have anything
in there today

00:44:42.630 --> 00:44:44.700
but it is something we're
definitely looking at.

00:44:44.700 --> 00:44:46.680
But feel free to use
it with GraphQL today.

00:44:46.680 --> 00:44:48.270
You will find value
in that and we've

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:50.730
seen a few patterns on how to
support it well with Apigee.

00:44:50.730 --> 00:44:51.360
GREG BRAIL: Right.

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:53.152
Can all of this be
configured via framework

00:44:53.152 --> 00:44:55.570
such as Serverless or arc.codes?

00:44:55.570 --> 00:44:57.300
I don't know what arc.codes is.

00:44:57.300 --> 00:44:59.810
I don't think Serverless
is working with Apigee yet.

00:44:59.810 --> 00:45:01.410
But we've had people
talk about it.

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:03.955
Everything in Apigee
however, is done via API.

00:45:03.955 --> 00:45:04.830
KEITH DANEKIND: Yeah.

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:07.740
GREG BRAIL: So what many people
have done-- so for instance,

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:10.830
you showed doing things with
a GUI but when most people do

00:45:10.830 --> 00:45:13.800
is there's a Maven plug-in and
an AMP plug-- well, maybe not.

00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:14.820
Nobody uses AMP anymore.

00:45:14.820 --> 00:45:17.130
There's a Jenkins plug-in.

00:45:17.130 --> 00:45:19.680
A lot of people have prebuilt
integrations with the APIs

00:45:19.680 --> 00:45:23.210
to automate the process of
defining APIs and pushing them

00:45:23.210 --> 00:45:23.710
out.

00:45:23.710 --> 00:45:26.550
And that's an example of
how you might do that.

00:45:26.550 --> 00:45:28.860
KEITH DANEKIND: Everything
that is in the UI is also

00:45:28.860 --> 00:45:30.630
supported through API.

00:45:30.630 --> 00:45:33.000
So our UI is built
on top of those APIs.

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:34.800
So you can also use
those to automate that

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:37.820
and a lot of people will
tie that into their CI/CD.

00:45:37.820 --> 00:45:38.780
GREG BRAIL: Yeah.

00:45:38.780 --> 00:45:41.090
How do I get on board
the Apigee hybrid beta?

00:45:41.090 --> 00:45:43.335
We should have a [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:43.335 --> 00:45:45.710
KEITH DANEKIND: Come talk to
us in the hallway afterwards

00:45:45.710 --> 00:45:47.863
and I'll give you
an answer that.

00:45:47.863 --> 00:45:48.780
GREG BRAIL: Let's see.

00:45:48.780 --> 00:45:51.660
Can Apigee be used to manage
a large inventory of APIs

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:55.200
with thousands of internal
users versus external users.

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:56.700
We've definitely
had people do that.

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:57.560
KEITH DANEKIND: We've had
a lot of people do that.

00:45:57.560 --> 00:45:58.860
GREG BRAIL: We have customers
who have hundreds of thousands

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:00.865
of consumers of their APIs.

00:46:03.747 --> 00:46:05.830
KEITH DANEKIND: Does Apigee
have a circuit breaker

00:46:05.830 --> 00:46:07.240
for services that slow down?

00:46:07.240 --> 00:46:08.440
It certainly does.

00:46:08.440 --> 00:46:11.590
We've got a spike
arrest policy in there.

00:46:11.590 --> 00:46:13.690
That can be inserted in
there so that if it's

00:46:13.690 --> 00:46:16.843
called too frequently it
will shut them down and use

00:46:16.843 --> 00:46:18.010
the circuit breaker pattern.

00:46:18.010 --> 00:46:18.190
GREG BRAIL: Right.

00:46:18.190 --> 00:46:20.410
Someone asked about
using Apigee as a proxy

00:46:20.410 --> 00:46:23.527
between internal microservices
as well as publishing them.

00:46:23.527 --> 00:46:25.360
There are actually a
couple ways to do that.

00:46:25.360 --> 00:46:27.940
There is a version of
the API gateway called

00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:30.340
the micro gateway,
which people sometimes

00:46:30.340 --> 00:46:33.490
co-located with lots of services
so that they can run it.

00:46:33.490 --> 00:46:35.590
They can have lots of
little API gateways instead

00:46:35.590 --> 00:46:36.790
of a few big ones.

00:46:36.790 --> 00:46:39.580
Also, there is integration
between Apigee and Istio.

00:46:39.580 --> 00:46:41.633
So if you're running
an Istio service mesh--

00:46:41.633 --> 00:46:43.300
and if there's no one
at this conference

00:46:43.300 --> 00:46:45.383
running Istio or service
mesh, we would definitely

00:46:45.383 --> 00:46:46.460
have them here.

00:46:46.460 --> 00:46:49.780
You can actually plug
Apigee into the Istio Mixer

00:46:49.780 --> 00:46:53.230
so that once Istio is managing
your services in the mesh,

00:46:53.230 --> 00:46:56.380
you can use Apigee for quota
for API key verification.

00:46:56.380 --> 00:46:59.050
And we're making some
progress on OAuth as well.

00:46:59.050 --> 00:47:00.940
The use case for that
is I have a number

00:47:00.940 --> 00:47:02.530
of services running in Istio--

00:47:02.530 --> 00:47:05.480
and Istio, by the way as a
part of the new cloud services

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:08.170
platform that has a name
that I've already forgotten.

00:47:08.170 --> 00:47:08.980
I'm sorry.

00:47:08.980 --> 00:47:10.570
But anyway, Apigee.

00:47:10.570 --> 00:47:11.710
Istio is part of that.

00:47:11.710 --> 00:47:14.590
If you have Istio and you have
services in the Istio mesh

00:47:14.590 --> 00:47:17.230
let's imagine you want to turn
one or two of those services

00:47:17.230 --> 00:47:21.970
into APIs, publish them to
users outside that service mesh,

00:47:21.970 --> 00:47:26.410
and use the developer portals or
quotas API keys and OAuth stuff

00:47:26.410 --> 00:47:28.790
that we showed you
in the demos today.

00:47:28.790 --> 00:47:30.760
You can do that
in Istio right now

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:34.810
by integrating Istio with Apigee
whether Apigee is on premise,

00:47:34.810 --> 00:47:35.960
hybrid, or in the cloud.

